+The executable development-cell owner marks cleanup complete only after network
+retirement and runtime-directory removal both succeed. A failed network deletion
+retains that object and every lower ownership step; a failed forwarding restore
+retains its recovery record. Completed deletions are not replayed by the same
+owner, and a record-removal retry does not repeat a completed global sysctl write.
+
+After a child wait or cleanup failure, `droidloomd` keeps the old cell handle and
+returns `ok: false` with state `cleanup_failed`. Routine polling does not retry
+effects or release the slot. Start, restart, launches and readiness operations
+remain blocked; an authorized explicit stop, or final daemon shutdown, may retry
+the remaining cleanup. Only successful cleanup releases the slot. The daemon
+keeps its listener alive after a polling failure so that failure cannot become
+an automatic restart with an empty owner slot. Consumers must understand the new
+state; an older strict protocol client will reject it rather than report success.
+
+This retention applies while the daemon remains alive. Forced daemon termination,
+proof that a prior namespace has fully retired, and recovery-record replay across
+daemon generations require separate lifecycle recovery qualification. Retaining
+the forwarding record does not by itself establish those properties.
+
